WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.338 --> 00:00:10.020
[Music] I think the role that I have helped&nbsp;
bring to this space is bringing people together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:15.840
and so I think through my work at Posit I saw&nbsp;
that so many of these pharmaceutical companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.920
just wanted to get together. They wanted to talk&nbsp;
and they were coming to me to say can you make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:25.200
these introductions so starting R and Pharma&nbsp;
was really the spark that brought a lot of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:30.360
pharmaceutical companies together and there&nbsp;
for the first time the R validation hub had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:36.000
their hangout, uh you know ideas around the pharmaverse were sparked out of the idea that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.940
pharmaceutical companies had specific workflows
and code that they wanted to share, so it's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:47.820
amazing to really just see the life that has&nbsp;
come out of the R and Pharma conference. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:54.420
was very much this sense and momentum that a new&nbsp;
horizon was coming out for open source to change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:59.760
the landscape and lead to a lot of innovations&nbsp;
for clinical trials, and so a lot of groups that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:06.060
had implemented R for research or for Shiny had&nbsp;
now started saying can we use this in addition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:11.100
for our late stage clinical trials work. I could&nbsp;
certainly feel that there was a change coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:17.220
to the way that clinical trials were conducted&nbsp;
in bringing open source to that ecosystem but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.460
I knew that it was going to take some time and&nbsp;
there was going to be a decent amount of change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:25.560
management that would have to take place for&nbsp;
that to work. Part of the popularity of R in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:31.200
the drug development space, especially late stage&nbsp;
clinical trials, is because there was a legacy stat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:37.020
programming language that people had used and so&nbsp;
migrating from that language to R was a very easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:43.380
transition for a lot of organizations because R&nbsp;
is an open source language built for statistical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:49.080
computing, as well as that legacy code and&nbsp;
programming tool was also built for stats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:54.600
programming, so I think the transition from that&nbsp;
previous tool to R is natural because languages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:59.700
like Python are more general purpose programming&nbsp;
languages and so I think that has led to a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:04.740
of the adoption in the space as well as Shiny I&nbsp;
would say. A lot of pharmaceutical companies use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:10.440
our professional software for their clinical&nbsp;
trials and that typically consists of Posit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:15.780
Team and Posit Team is made up of three different&nbsp;
products - there's a Posit Workbench which is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:19.920
development environment, literally it's called a&nbsp;
development environment because it's where people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.780
are going to go in and do their programming. There&nbsp;
they create software and artifacts and content and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:29.520
Shiny apps and workflows, and then they move that&nbsp;
into production, into our second product which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.500
called Posit Connect, that's the production and&nbsp;
deployment platform that we host and maintain,&nbsp;

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:39.660
and then the back end, what's surfacing out the&nbsp;
production, and oftentimes validated packages&nbsp;,&nbsp;

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:44.760
for both of those environments is the Posit&nbsp;
Package Manager, and so that's feeding packages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:50.220
for reproducibility into the development side, as&nbsp;
well as feeding packages into the production side.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:57.240
Pharmaceutical companies care a lot about Posit
Package Manager because there is this great desire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:02.700
to own and manage the validation and oftentimes&nbsp;
what's referred to as qualification of packages,&nbsp;

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:06.780
and so a lot of work goes internally at&nbsp;
pharmaceutical companies to say these are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:12.060
the core packages, typically around 100 to&nbsp;
2 or 300 or so packages, that this is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:18.360
people are going to use for clinical trials,&nbsp;
so package manager specifically is critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:23.400
for clinical development because it makes the&nbsp;
source of where their package is coming from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:29.460
locked down and approved for the work internally&nbsp;
at the company. Yeah, there's a lot of use cases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:35.400
and companies and pharmaceutical organizations&nbsp;
using our professional tools. Recently this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:43.260
highlighted by Satish Murthy in the data science&nbsp;
hangout that they use Posit Package Manager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:48.660
for surfacing and managing the source of their&nbsp;
validated packages that they bring out - that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:53.340
one of the most key things for organizations&nbsp;
is to make sure the source of the package is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.340 --> 00:04:00.180
coming into the workflows is validated and the&nbsp;
right packages. In addition to that, Merck also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:04.320
had a webinar with us that highlighted their&nbsp;
use of Package Manager. There's lots of other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:09.960
organizations that have highlighted the use of&nbsp;
Posit Connect for where they manage and host&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:15.480
their Shiny applications, for example Roche and&nbsp;
Genentech in their webinar a couple months ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:20.100
highlighted that they have an entire framework&nbsp;
that they've built in-house around Shiny, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:24.660
they host their Shiny apps there with Posit&nbsp;
Connect. It's going to be interesting to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:32.520
how the future plays out with introductions to new&nbsp;
tooling like Python and leading that into AI, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:36.060
how some of that could change some of the clinical&nbsp;
trials. I don't think that's going to happen right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:42.000
away but we see this very big inclusive and&nbsp;
interoperable future for drug development

